## What was missing and is now done

Step 4 — the CLAUDE.md paragraph. The gate could fail cargo test and trigger `git reset --hard` on a whole task while being invisible to the file re-injected as authoritative context every session. It now sits at lines 96–104, directly after the seventh sibling (tests/cargo_spawning_tests.rs) and before the mutation-testing section, carrying: the measured reason (#863's 14 free consumers vs 11 silent bypasses, and why that honest disclosure was durable debt rather than a discharge), the rule, both opposite directions plus the empty-reason and anti-vacuous branches, the dated census, the four limits, and an explicit statement that it enumerates and does not fix — #864 stays open. grep -c "^\*\*.*gate\*\* (\tests/"` now returns 8 where it returned 7.

## The defect I found while writing it

The gate's own module doc said "Three limits" and listed three, while write_census printed four — the fourth being that the register key is (path, fn), not (path, line). Documenting four in CLAUDE.md without correcting that would have created a three-way disagreement between the doc, the code, and the output, which is precisely the stale-claim class this repo keeps paying for. I corrected the module doc and the write_census doc comment instead of quietly writing "three" everywhere.

That fourth limit is load-bearing rather than cosmetic, and I measured it rather than trusting the register's prose: commands_search.rs carries 7 non-test sites under only 4 entries, because run_grep (1191+1200), run_grep_count (1286+1295) and run_grep_with_context (1399+1408) each stand for two sites. So a second bypass added to an already-registered fn is inherited silently — stated in both the doc and CLAUDE.md as a deliberate trade (a line key would fire on every unrelated line-shifting edit, which is the churn that trains a reader to paste past a gate without reading it).

